Control, same rig, same build, `rotate` (an organize-surface tool with no password
field of its own) on the AES-128 fixture:

  run   : clicked <button> Rotate
  result: AES-128-USER.PDF"aes-128-user.pdf" is password protected.
  notes : … / Enter the password for "aes-128-user.pdf" and run again.

and the identical outcome on the AES-256 fixture. So the missing gate on that
surface is not an AES-256 defect and not a regression from the revision-6 read
path: it is how the organize workspace asks (or fails to ask) for a secret when
pdf.js can already preview the file. `unlock`, which carries its own password
field, is the surface this proof runs on, and it is a rewriting tool: the
downloaded bytes above are its output, judged by MuPDF and libqpdf.
